uggests  to call this  a metaphysical commitment instead of  arguments from beauty  yes that's how you can think of it you  know i i prefer to talk about beauty  because it's  a somewhat more digestible word um i  also think that  um it's it's um ultimately where these  arguments come from  it's the reason why physicists would  want nature to be in this particular way  is because it would be more beautiful  but metaphysical assumptions is exactly  what it is these are assumptions about  nature  that themselves are not based on  evidence their beliefs basically  um so what's the consequence of this  well um there are lots of  failed predictions that have come out of  this belief that nature has to be  beautiful  in very specific mathematical ways i  will now  briefly go through some of those for  example the standard model contains a  number which is called the  theta parameter which is small and  physicists think that small numbers are  ugly so they don't like that  they've tried to make this theory  prettier um  in a certain specific way that doesn't  really matter for our purposes  and this prettier theory predicts a new  particle which was called the axion  this particle was proposed and ruled out  very quickly  after its proposal in the 1970s  but what then happened and this is  symptomatic what uh  to what would happen over and over again  physicists did not just abandon this  idea of the axiom  but instead they amended the theory  um so that they had a new axiom which  was more difficult to  observe and this was called the  invisible axiom  actually if you look at the literature  today um they've just dropped the term  axion and a lot of especially the  younger students don't even know that  um this axion that they're dealing with  today is not the original  axion there have been a lot of  experiments looking for these  axions and you see one in this  image in the corner so far  nobody has seen an axiom the trouble is  they're invisible so this  theory was deliberately designed so that  it  is difficult to test  another one of the failed predictions is  that as i briefly mentioned the standard  model  is not as symmetric as it could be i  have a little graph of the standard  model you know you have all these  particles that kind of they have some  structure but it's not  particularly great you know it could be  better you know  i can think of ways to make it better  other people have thought of smarter  ways to make it better  and these are the grand unified theories  um they're arguably pretty  um now the thing is that once you have a  theory  of that type it um predicts some new  interactions and those have generically  the consequence that protons are  unstable  they decay now you can look for this you  can look at protons for example water is  basically  made of protons so what you do to test  this  is you very closely monitor large  amounts of water  to see if there are any protons decaying  uh so far no one has seen a single  proton decay  this does not entirely rule out these  theories um it has ruled out some of  them  um but there are always ways that you  can fiddle with these  theories so that um the proton is a  little bit longer lived  so it is not affected by the  observations that we have  so far um then  many of you will probably remember that  the large hadron collider which you see  in this image  um we're supposed to see other things  besides the exposure there was a lot of  talk about  supersymmetric particles or maybe tiny  black holes additional dimensions  those were supposed to show up at the  large hadron collider  why did so many physicists think that  this  should be the case well it's that the  standard model is ugly  in a very specific way besides the theta  parameter it contains another number  that is small which is the mass  of the higgs boson or more precisely  it's the mass of the exposure divided by  something called the planck mass  that comes out to be very small and  physicists don't like that  so what you can do is you can make this  theory prettier  for example by adding supersymmetry to  it or additional dimensions  and because that theory is prettier  physicists thought  that well nature should go for it and  we should see these things that the  larch hadn't collided it turned out  to not work the only new particle that  was seen was  the higgs boson that was predicted in  the 1960s  and i have to emphasize that this was  not a prediction  um based on an argument from beauty it  was a prediction based  on inconsistency um without the higgs  boson the standard model is just  inconsistent it stops working at  energies that the lhc can reach  another failed prediction um is for a  specific type of particles that  are supposed to make up dark matter  these are the so-called weakly  interacting massive particles  um these have been sought for since the  mid 1980s  uh so far none have been seen each time  time and experiment comes back  empty-handed theorists go and here  it it happens again and make their  theories more complicated so that the  particles um  are more difficult to detect what you  see in this little  image in the corner is a dark matter  detector called xenon 1t  this is an upgrade from xenon 100 which  is an upgrade from xenon 10 which is an  upgrade of the original xenon experiment  in each upgrade the sensitivity of the  detectors was increased they still  haven't  seen the thing why did physicists  believe in that  well it's because the wimps are supposed  to explain a numerical coincidence that  has um gone down in the literature under  the name wimp  miracle um that's basically  um the coincidence that um  particles of a certain mass that is in  the range  of the large hadron collider would have  been produced in the early universe in  about the right amount to make  up for the observed effects of dark  matter  um so you know that that may just be  coincidence  but um theorist theoreticians  in uh the foundations believe that  there's something more here than  than just a mere coincidence because if  it was just coincidence that would be  ugly  um then in general relativity we have  also um  a constant um that is very small  emphasis think it's ugly that's the  previously mentioned cosmological  constant  um again you can try to make this theory  prettier  by um making it more complicated and  trying to do without this constant um  usually the way that you do this  is by converting the constant into some  kind of dynamical field  that changes during the evolution of the  universe  um you can look for these kind of fields  um  at least for some of them and to the  extent that it has been possible to look  for them  and they were not found  so um i think this this gives you some  idea of  um why i think it's not a good idea to  um rely on arguments from beauty it just  has um  worked very badly for physicists in the  last 40 years  so they haven't given up on it so why do  physicists even believe in beauty  i think um basically the reason is that  they don't think about what they're  doing as i said earlier these are  metaphysical  assumptions and this is just something  that uh physicists don't like to talk  about they don't even  write down that it's an assumption that  they are making  for the most part they actually leave it  a belief it's just  a mathematical criterion that they are  using  another part of the problem is that if  you try to pin them down on it  many of them will claim that it's  justified because the iraq and einstein  were successful  because they were guided by beauty and  maybe that's the story that you have  heard too  unfortunately it's um to begin with it's  wrong  um dirac and einstein certainly spoke  about beauty  but the reason that they ultimately were  successful as we know  know is um that they were  solving problems that actually required  a solution  they were both um facing inconsistencies  in the  theories at the time einstein was  facing the inconsistency between special  relativity and newtonian gravity  and dirac was looking at the  inconsistency between  special relativity and the then existing  formulation of quantum theory  and so they had a good problem to work  on it has it has  it had nothing to do with beauty really  and even if  it was right that it was beauty that led  them  to success that's cherry-picked data you  also have to look at the cases where  arguments from beauty did not work  and then and on that score you only have  to look at iraq and einstein themselves  because  after they had made their big successes  they tried to use arguments from beauty  um to develop some  fundamental great theory of everything  and it did not  work indeed  if you look at the history of physics  it's pretty clear that arguments from  beauty have just worked  badly so here's a brief selection of  beautiful ideas that just  didn't work there was this idea from  kepler  that the orbits of um the then known  five planets are determined by the way  that you can stack  certain types of polyhedra in inside  each other you see this in this  image from wikipedia but that just  turned out to be wrong  for various reasons um notably because  we now know that there are more than  five planets and also their orbits are  not circular  you know i i don't think it's a kind of  beauty that most people would even find  beautiful  um today um the circular orbits  themselves  were something that physicists were very  reluctant to give up on  because they thought well you know the  circle is the most beautiful  motion that you can think of so  certainly  if god had any choice making the last of  nature then he would have chosen the  circle  this is where this whole story with the  epicycles uh come from  where um people didn't want to give up  uh on  on these circles even when it didn't fit  on the data so they made circles around  circles around circles  again this is an idea of beauty that i  think no one would actually defend today  there was an episode in the history of  physics where  it became quite popular to believe that  um  atoms are really certain kinds of knots  uh in an invisible ether and i've  i have here an image of a certain kind  of plot as you can see it has a certain  aesthetic appeal i  i won't deny it and the theory also  turned out to be just wrong  um and for a long time just to add one  final example  it was considered to be beautiful that  the universe is in an external  in eternally unchanging state it just  has always been this way and will always  continue to be this way  uh today we also know that this is just  wrong  and then i already briefly mentioned  that both drock and einstein  uh in their late years tried to develop  their  um grand unified series of everything um  and  it worked badly and they were certainly  not the only one there were there were  many other people like  um iceberg uh ellington um and so on who  tried the same thing and it just  uh did not work and on the other hand we  have ugly ideas that  work just fine best example i could  think of is this fly trap  definitely ugly um but works quite well  quantum mechanics for example a lot of  my colleagues tend to find quantum  mechanics ugly um  i don't think myself that it is ugly um  but  you know in the end it's subjective to  who am i to disagree with them  whether or not you think that it's ugly  it works very well  in quantum field theory we have a  procedure called regularization  that we need to coax out  predictions from the mathematics a lot  of people think that this is ugly  um dirac for example was very opposed to  that whatever you think about it  it works just fine and then i already  mentioned that we have those elliptical  orbits  for the planets that were originally  considered to be very ugly  and the big bang theory of the universe  um there um so in in  in summary um you can say well there's  just no correlation between beauty and  explanatory power  um and there's also 